(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-vascular-disorders-vignettes"]={"title":"Pulmonary Vascular Disorders Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who takes an estrogen-containing oral contraceptive has had 1 day of pleuritic chest pain and has coughed up a small amount of blood once. Her heart rate is 106/min and oxygen saturation 98%. Her legs show no swelling or tenderness, she has had no surgery or immobilization, she has had no previous clots and has no cancer, and the clinician judges viral pleurisy more likely than pulmonary embolism. What is her Wells score?","choices":["Score of 1.5","Score of 2.5","Score of 3.5","Score of 5.5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart rate above 100 (106/min) 1.5 + hemoptysis 1 + signs of deep vein thrombosis 0 + embolism more likely than alternatives 0 + surgery or immobilization 0 + previous clot 0 + malignancy 0 = 2.5. Estrogen use is a risk factor and a PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) item, but it is not on the Wells list. A score of 4 or less means embolism is unlikely. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 49, 53"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has received parenteral nutrition through a central venous catheter for 5 weeks and now has a pulmonary embolism. Which part of Virchow's triad does the catheter contribute?","choices":["Endothelial injury","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulability","Arterial occlusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central venous catheters, like pacemaker wires, recent surgery and pelvic or lower-limb trauma, contribute endothelial injury in Virchow's triad. The catheter damages the wall of the vein it sits in, giving clot a place to start. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cor pulmonale uses continuous home oxygen. He has had three exacerbations this year, each set off by a respiratory infection. Besides the oxygen, which plan addresses the root of his cor pulmonale?","choices":["Thrombolysis and anticoagulation","Strict fluid restriction alone","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor therapy","Bronchodilators and prompt infection care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The prime directive in cor pulmonale is to treat the underlying lung disease, because the right ventricle cannot recover while the lungs keep loading it. For chronic obstructive pulmonary disease that means bronchodilators and aggressive treatment of respiratory infections, the triggers of his exacerbations, alongside continuous oxygen. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with a confirmed pulmonary embolism becomes confused and clammy. Her blood pressure has been 76/48 mmHg for 40 minutes despite fluids. She has had no recent surgery, no bleeding and no stroke. How is her embolism risk-stratified, and what follows for treatment?","choices":["Submassive; anticoagulation alone","Massive; anticoagulation alone","Low risk; outpatient anticoagulation","Massive; systemic thrombolysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sustained hypotension (here 76/48 mmHg for 40 minutes) defines massive, high-risk embolism, a circulatory collapse with high mortality that requires immediate advanced intervention. Thrombolysis is the standard for massive, hemodynamically unstable embolism, and she has no stated contraindication. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension, functional class II. During right heart catheterization her pulmonary pressures fall markedly on vasoreactivity testing, so she is vasoreactive. Which therapy fits her?","choices":["High-dose oral calcium channel blocker","Continuous intravenous prostanoid","Early oral combination therapy","Lung transplantation referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vasoreactivity testing splits the treatment path: patients who respond are treated with high-dose oral calcium channel blockers. Patients who are not vasoreactive are the ones who move on to risk stratification and the targeted pulmonary arterial hypertension classes. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has progressive exertional dyspnea. Echocardiography estimates a high pulmonary artery systolic pressure with an enlarged right ventricle, and her chest film, pulmonary function tests and ventilation-perfusion scan are normal. Which test is required to confirm pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Computed tomographic angiography","B-type natriuretic peptide level","Right heart catheterization","Repeat echocardiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Right heart catheterization is the mandatory confirmation: it measures the mean pulmonary artery pressure, the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and pulmonary vascular resistance. A high mean pressure with a normal wedge pressure confirms pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 24, 36"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and chronic hypoxemia has a hematocrit of 58% (normal 41-50%). Echocardiography shows right ventricular hypertrophy. How does his blood count add to the load on his right ventricle?","choices":["It lowers oxygen content","It raises blood viscosity","It lowers blood viscosity","It dilates pulmonary arteries"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic hypoxia drives polycythemia, and the extra red cells raise blood viscosity and the workload of the heart. That compounds the structural and hypoxic rise in pulmonary resistance his right ventricle already pumps against. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man had a pulmonary embolism 4 months ago and still has exertional dyspnea despite anticoagulation. A ventilation-perfusion scan shows persistent mismatched perfusion defects. Which step fits next?","choices":["Repeat the scan in a year","Systemic thrombolysis now","Refer to an expert center","Stop anticoagulation and reassess"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abnormal perfusion after 3 months of anticoagulation leads to transthoracic echocardiography, right heart catheterization and pulmonary angiography, then referral to an expert center to evaluate chronic thromboembolic disease. Anticoagulation continues during the workup, and the optimal treatment for chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension is surgical: pulmonary thromboendarterectomy. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has had chronic obstructive pulmonary disease for 15 years, with slowly progressive exertional dyspnea and a chronic unproductive cough. Echocardiography shows a thick-walled right ventricle with preserved function. Which right ventricular response does this reflect?","choices":["Acute dilation and stretch","Microinfarction from wall tension","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Concentric hypertrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lung disease raises pulmonary pressure gradually over months to years, which gives the right ventricle time to adapt: it thickens with concentric hypertrophy and holds its function until eventual exhaustion and late dilation. Her thick wall with preserved function after 15 years fits that compensated stage. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema. He is sitting upright on high-flow oxygen and has received an intravenous loop diuretic, but he remains tachypneic with an oxygen saturation of 87%. He is alert and able to protect his airway. Which step comes next?","choices":["Intravenous fluid bolus","Lying flat to rest","Routine intravenous morphine","Noninvasive ventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation is the P in the toolbox: it mechanically stents flooded alveoli open, pushes fluid back across the membrane and lowers intrathoracic preload and afterload. He is alert and can protect his airway, and oxygen with diuresis has not been enough. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 32, 39, 40"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has had exertional dyspnea and fatigue for 10 months. Two clinicians told her she was out of shape, but echocardiography now shows a high estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure and an enlarged right ventricle, and catheterization confirms pulmonary arterial hypertension. She asks why it was missed. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Early disease causes syncope first","Echocardiography misses early disease","Early symptoms mimic deconditioning","The disease starts with chest pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exertional dyspnea and fatigue are often the only early symptoms of pulmonary hypertension, and they are frequently misattributed to deconditioning. That is why the disease is often found late, after months of being told to get fitter. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 21, 24"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has ankle edema. When the examiner applies sustained, moderate pressure over her liver, her jugular venous pressure rises by 3 cm and stays up. What does this hepatojugular reflux indicate?","choices":["Low right-sided filling pressure","Low systemic vascular resistance","Elevated right-sided filling pressure","Elevated left atrial filling pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hepatojugular reflux is a rise in jugular venous pressure of more than 1 cm with sustained, moderate pressure on the liver, and it correlates with elevated right-sided filling pressures. In pulmonary hypertension it marks a right ventricle that cannot take the extra venous return. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man becomes suddenly short of breath, sweaty and pale and faints in the airport after a 14-hour flight. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle with a thin wall, and computed tomographic pulmonary angiography shows a massive pulmonary embolism. He has no lung disease. Which right ventricular response does this presentation reflect?","choices":["Concentric hypertrophy, then dilation","Compensatory hypertrophy","Gradual exhaustion of reserve","Acute dilation and dysfunction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A massive embolism raises pressure faster than the right ventricle can adapt: wall tension rises instantly and the chamber dilates and fails within minutes to hours. Severe sudden dyspnea, sweating, pallor and syncope are the acute pattern, and the thin wall shows there was no time for hypertrophy. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has had interstitial lung disease for 8 years, and his right ventricle has been known to be hypertrophied. Over the past month his exertional dyspnea has worsened. Which new finding shows that his right ventricle has decompensated?","choices":["Flattened diaphragms on the film","Pink frothy sputum","Tender hepatomegaly with ascites","Orthopnea with bibasilar crackles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When a chronically hypertrophied right ventricle decompensates, its elevated diastolic pressure transmits backward: the liver congests and becomes tender, ascites forms and pitting edema spreads in dependent areas. A right ventricular heave, an intensified P2 and a tricuspid regurgitation murmur go with it. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 23, 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with ischemic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (ejection fraction 25%) is admitted with acute pulmonary edema after gaining 6 kg of fluid. His blood pressure is 138/84 mmHg and his creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (normal 0.7-1.3). Which diuresis strategy fits him?","choices":["Aggressive diuresis","Gentle, minimal diuresis","Withholding diuretics for now","Fluid challenge first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adults with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, usually ischemia-driven, have more physiological reserve, and aggressive diuresis is generally well tolerated. With a large fluid gain, a stable blood pressure and normal kidney function, he can have fluid removed briskly. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 39, 42"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has exertional dyspnea and signs of right ventricular failure. His chest film and pulmonary function tests are normal, a ventilation-perfusion scan shows several mismatched segmental perfusion defects, and he had a pulmonary embolism 2 years ago. Which World Health Organization pulmonary hypertension group fits?","choices":["Group 1","Group 2","Group 3","Group 4"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normal lung studies rule out lung disease (Group 3), and segmental perfusion defects after a past embolism point to unresolved clot burden. That is chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension, the obstructive pre-capillary Group 4. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 12, 19"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension faints while climbing two flights of stairs. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle, a septum bowing into the left ventricle and normal left ventricular contractility. What explains her exertional syncope?","choices":["Right ventricular hypertrophy alone","A drop in pulmonary resistance","Impaired left ventricular filling","Weak left ventricular contraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In advanced pulmonary hypertension the dilated right ventricle pushes the septum into the left ventricle, so the left ventricle fills poorly in diastole and output falls, even though its contractility is normal. Syncope on exertion is ominous: it signals a fixed low output that cannot rise with effort. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 20, 21"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with pulmonary arterial hypertension has no signs of right ventricular failure. His symptoms have progressed moderately over the past year, he has fainted once, he is in World Health Organization functional class III, and his six-minute walk distance is 310 m. Which prognostic risk band fits him?","choices":["Low risk, under 5% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, 5-10% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, over 10% at 1 year","High risk, over 10% at 1 year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each feature sits in the middle row: no right ventricular failure signs, moderate progression, occasional syncope, functional class III and a six-minute walk distance between 165 and 440 m. Intermediate risk carries an estimated 1-year mortality of 5-10%. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has 2 days of cough with small streaks of blood and mild dyspnea. He had a deep vein thrombosis 3 years ago. His heart rate is 108/min and oxygen saturation 96%, and his legs show no swelling or tenderness. He has had no recent surgery or immobilization and has no cancer. Because he has a fever and a focal infiltrate, the clinician judges pneumonia more likely than pulmonary embolism. What is his Wells score?","choices":["Score of 2.5","Score of 4","Score of 5.5","Score of 7"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Previous deep vein thrombosis 1.5 + heart rate above 100 (108/min) 1.5 + hemoptysis 1 + signs of deep vein thrombosis 0 + embolism more likely than alternatives 0 (pneumonia is judged more likely) + surgery or immobilization 0 + malignancy 0 = 4. A score of 4 or less means embolism is unlikely. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure has cardiogenic pulmonary edema. She is drowsy, and her arterial carbon dioxide is 66 mmHg (normal 35-45). Which agent from the acute toolbox carries the greatest risk for her?","choices":["Noninvasive ventilation","Morphine","Intravenous nitrate","Intravenous loop diuretic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Morphine is a use-with-caution agent: it offers venodilation and eases anxiety, but it risks respiratory depression and severe reduction of ventilatory drive. In a drowsy patient already retaining carbon dioxide, that risk outweighs the benefit. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 39, 40, 46"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has had mild left-sided chest wall pain for a day, reproduced by pressing on a rib cartilage. His Wells score is 0, and the clinician's gestalt for pulmonary embolism is very low (under 15%). His heart rate is 78/min and oxygen saturation 99%. He has no hemoptysis, takes no estrogen, has never had a clot, has had no recent surgery or trauma and has no leg swelling. Applying the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria), which step fits?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan","Stop: no further testing","Order a D-dimer to be safe","Computed tomographic angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He meets all eight criteria: age under 50, heart rate under 100, saturation above 94%, no hemoptysis, no estrogen, no prior venous thromboembolism, no surgery or trauma and no unilateral leg swelling. With very low gestalt and every criterion met, the workup stops. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 57"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman has sudden dyspnea and a swollen, tender left leg, and her Wells score is 6. She had anaphylaxis to iodinated contrast during a previous scan. Her creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.1), and she has no chronic lung disease. Which test should confirm or exclude pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography","Bedside echocardiography","A high-sensitivity D-dimer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe contrast allergy is one of the reasons to choose the ventilation-perfusion scan over computed tomographic pulmonary angiography. With no chronic lung disease the scan is less likely to be nondiagnostic, and a completely negative scan excludes embolism with the same certainty as a negative pulmonary angiogram. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 55, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who has smoked for 50 years has 3 days of worsening dyspnea and wheezing. His chest film shows hyperinflation without vascular congestion, his arterial carbon dioxide is 61 mmHg (normal 35-45), and his B-type natriuretic peptide is 70 pg/mL (normal under 100). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Heart failure exacerbation","Obstructive lung disease flare","Acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Acute pulmonary embolism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Wheezing, hyperinflation on the chest film and hypercapnia point to an exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and the natriuretic peptide is usually normal in that setting, as his is. Dyspnea, hypoxemia and tachycardia are shared with heart failure and embolism, so these features decide. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension and is not vasoreactive on testing. She is in functional class II with stable symptoms and no signs of right ventricular failure. Which treatment approach fits her?","choices":["Early oral combination therapy","Oral monotherapy, adding drugs later","High-dose oral calcium channel blocker","Continuous intravenous prostanoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients who are not vasoreactive are stratified by risk, and low or intermediate risk (class II or III) calls for oral combination therapy: an endothelin receptor antagonist with a phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor. Starting the combination early is superior to sequential monotherapy. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 26, 28"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with no medical history, no medicines, no recent travel, surgery or immobilization and no family history of clots presents with sudden dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. His heart rate is 116/min and oxygen saturation 90% on room air, and his chest film is clear. Which approach fits?","choices":["Defer testing until a risk factor appears","Evaluate objectively for embolism","Exclude embolism: he has no risk factors","Treat for anxiety and reassess"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Failing to identify a known predisposition should never dissuade an objective evaluation when the presentation is consistent with embolism. Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia and a clear film fit embolism, risk factors or not. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 48, 49, 50"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a massive pulmonary embolism has had a blood pressure of 70/40 mmHg for 25 minutes. He also has active bleeding from a duodenal ulcer, with black stools and a hemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL). Which intervention fits?","choices":["Thrombectomy or embolectomy","Thrombolysis after transfusion","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Anticoagulation and observation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Massive embolism with sustained hypotension needs immediate advanced intervention, but active bleeding is an absolute contraindication to thrombolytics. Mechanical thrombectomy or surgical embolectomy physically removes the clot without a drug that would worsen his bleeding. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a pulmonary embolism has pleuritic chest pain and hemoptysis. His chest film shows a wedge-shaped, pleural-based opacity in the right lower zone. Which sign is this, and what does it indicate?","choices":["Hampton's hump, an infarction","Westermark sign, an infarction","Palla sign, an infarction","Hampton's hump, a pneumonia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hampton's hump is a wedge-shaped, pleural-based opacity that indicates pulmonary infarction, matching his pleuritic pain and hemoptysis from distal, pleural-based clot. Classic signs like this are highly suggestive of embolism but infrequent. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man inhaled chlorine gas in an industrial accident. Six hours later he has severe dyspnea and bilateral infiltrates. Echocardiography is normal, and his pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is 8 mmHg. Which mechanism best explains his pulmonary edema?","choices":["Diastolic stiffening of the ventricle","Regurgitant flow across a heart valve","Toxic injury to the capillary membrane","Hydrostatic backup from the left heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled toxins cause chemical injury to the alveolar-capillary membrane, so the barrier shatters and protein-rich fluid leaks into the alveoli: noncardiogenic, permeability edema. His normal echocardiogram and wedge pressure of 8 mmHg exclude a pressure problem. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 33, 34"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with interstitial lung disease, long-standing high blood pressure and type 2 diabetes develops ankle edema and a parasternal heave (lift along the left sternal border). Echocardiography shows an enlarged right ventricle. Before his right ventricular failure can be attributed to cor pulmonale, which finding must be excluded?","choices":["A restrictive pulmonary function pattern","Low oxygen saturation during exercise","Enlarged central pulmonary arteries","Elevated left-sided filling pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cor pulmonale means right ventricular change caused exclusively by a lung or pulmonary vascular disorder, with a normal wedge pressure. His high blood pressure and diabetes raise the chance of left heart disease, so elevated left-sided filling pressure (Group 2, left heart failure) must be excluded first. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 4, 13, 23, 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction develops pneumonia with sepsis, then bilateral alveolar infiltrates and severe hypoxemia. His echocardiogram is technically limited, and his B-type natriuretic peptide is moderately raised. The team cannot tell whether his edema is cardiogenic or from a leaking barrier. Which test settles the question?","choices":["A repeat chest film in 6 hours","Serial natriuretic peptide levels","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography","Pulmonary artery catheter wedge pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pulmonary artery catheter (right heart catheterization) is the ultimate hemodynamic arbiter: it directly measures left-sided filling pressure when the cause remains uncertain. The wedge looks forward to the left atrium; above 18-20 mmHg confirms cardiogenic failure, while a normal value points to a permeability leak. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 33, 36, 38"}]};
